    // The fuzzer will supply arbitrary addresses; we deploy a malicious helper that is a contract
    // to enable re-entrancy through the supportsToken() external call that airDrop relies on.
    function testFuzz_property(address attacker, address victim) public {
        // keep addresses nonzero and distinct to be in wide admissible domain
        vm.assume(attacker != address(0));
        vm.assume(victim != address(0));

        // Deploy a malicious Bank-like contract that will re-enter C.airDrop during supportsToken()
        // The attacker will be the caller of c.airDrop, but C.supportsToken calls Bank(msg.sender).supportsToken(),
        // so we need the attacker to be a contract implementing supportsToken that triggers re-entry.
        MaliciousBank mal = new MaliciousBank(c, victim);

        // Ensure attacker (which will be the malicious contract) has no tokens initially in C
        // and victim also has zero initial balance (we want the general property: caller with zero gets +20 only)
        // No need to set balances on the token contract side; tokenBalance is within c and starts at 0.

        // The attacker (mal) will call c.airDrop. We need to have mal invoke c.airDrop as msg.sender
        // so mal must be the tx sender. Use vm.prank to simulate mal being the EOA sender of the transaction.
        // However the contract mal will itself perform an external call to c.airDrop; so we simply call mal.attack{gas:...}()

        // Execute the attack: mal will call c.airDrop() as itself and attempt re-entrancy from supportsToken().
        // Use vm.prank to set an EOA as tx.origin-like caller? Not necessary: mal.attack() will be executed by this test
        // VM's caller is the test contract; to make mal the msg.sender when calling c.airDrop, mal itself must call c.airDrop.
        // So we simply invoke mal.doAirdrop(), which will perform the sequence from the attacker contract.

        // Call and ignore reverts; we only inspect final balances via getter.
        // Use try/catch to avoid bubbling reverts.
        try mal.doAirdrop() {
        } catch {
        }

        // General property to assert: in the correct contract, the attacker (mal) started with 0 and after a single
        // airDrop call should have exactly 20 tokens, and no other account (victim) should have nonzero balance.
        // We assert this single universal property via the public getters.
        uint attackerBalance = c.tokenBalance(address(mal));
        uint victimBalance = c.tokenBalance(victim);

        // The reference C (with reentrancy guard) will always leave attackerBalance == 20 and victimBalance == 0
        // after this single direct airDrop by attacker-contract that had zero before. The mutant lacking the lock
